# /etc/0trust/canary.env — warrant canary signing and freshness alarm
# Copy to /etc/0trust/canary.env, chmod 0600, chown root:root
#
# 0trust-canary-check.timer reads this file. It is optional (the unit uses
# EnvironmentFile=-), and every value below has a working default, so the
# alarm runs without it. What you actually come here for is ALERT_WEBHOOK.
#
# The SIGNER values are read by canary/scripts/generate.sh, which is run by a
# human, not by a timer — deliberately. A canary a machine can re-sign on its
# own proves nothing about whether the operator is still free to sign it.
# Putting the signing key on the server would give the box the power the whole
# document exists to demonstrate the operator still holds. Keep them here only
# if you sign on this host and understand that trade-off; otherwise export
# them in the shell for the one command:
#
#   export CANARY_SIGNER_NAME="Your Name"
#   export CANARY_SIGNER_KEY=<40-hex fingerprint>
#   sudo -E /opt/0trust/canary/scripts/generate.sh

# Where the published canary is served from. The alarm reads the deployed
# copy, not the one in the build directory.
CANARY_PATH=/var/www/0trust/canary.txt

# Where to shout when the canary goes past its deadline. Anything that accepts
# a POST body works: ntfy.sh, a Matrix webhook, signal-cli's HTTP bridge.
# Empty means the only notice is a wall(1) message and a failed unit, which
# nobody reads on a server they are not logged into.
ALERT_WEBHOOK=

# How long before the published "Next signing" date to start saying so.
# This is a notice, not an incident: the check still exits 0.
ALERT_LEAD=172800

# Fallback age limit, used ONLY when "Next signing:" cannot be read out of the
# document. Lateness is normally measured against the deadline the canary
# published for itself — a flat age limit fires on a canary being operated
# exactly as designed, and an alarm that cries wolf weekly is one the operator
# stops reading.
ALERT_MAX_AGE=1209600
